module shell_namesake.pos_1

// A HEAP RECORD NAMED LIKE A STRUCT THE SHELL ALREADY DEFINES (D381, wave
// 274.5 pre68). The shell -- the oracle's emission of the probe, glued into
// every unit novac emits -- carries std's `struct Nova_DirEntry { ... }` (fs)
// AND the vector instance over it, `Nova_Vec____Nova_DirEntry_p`. A program's
// own `type DirEntry` is legal (D125: the user wins), and until this wave novac
// refused it by name, because its C struct would have been a second
// `struct Nova_DirEntry` in one unit and clang refuses that.
//
// D381 gives the colliding name a module-qualified base: novac spells this
// type `Nova_shell_namesake_pos_1_DirEntry`. The oracle compiles the same file
// with no std `DirEntry` in its unit, so it keeps the bare name -- the C differs,
// the behaviour must not: the lines below, byte for byte.
//
// What is exercised: construction, a field read, a method, a value passed to a
// function and returned from one, a vector of them. The vector is the sharp
// edge: spelled with the bare name, `[]DirEntry` would be the SHELL's instance
// over std's record -- a vector of somebody else's type that links fine. The
// in-name spelling goes through the same base, so novac makes its own instance.
//
// Why not `Outcome` (also a shell struct): the ORACLE fails a user `Outcome`
// beside `[]Outcome` -- std's prelude `Outcome[T]` is in its unit and the user
// type takes its place (registry 221.1 #1545).
type DirEntry {
    code int
    label str
}

fn DirEntry @doubled() -> int => @code * 2

fn make(code int, label str) -> DirEntry => { code, label }

fn bump(o DirEntry) -> DirEntry => { code: o.code + 1, label: o.label }

fn describe(o DirEntry) -> str => "${o.label}=${o.code}"

fn main() {
    ro a = make(3, "x")
    println(describe(a))
    ro b = bump(a)
    println(describe(b))
    println(b.doubled())
    mut all = []DirEntry.new()
    all.push(a)
    all.push(b)
    mut sum = 0
    for o in all {
        sum += o.code
    }
    println(sum)
}
